            "output": "<path>/workspace/apis/python/src/tiledbsoma/_arrow_types.py</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Copyright (c) 2021-2023 The Chan Zuckerberg Initiative Foundation\n2: # Copyright (c) 2021-2023 TileDB, Inc.\n3: #\n4: # Licensed under the MIT License.\n5: \n6: \"\"\"Conversion to/from Arrow and TileDB type systems. Must be capable\n7: of representing full type semantics, and correctly performing a\n8: round trip conversion (e.g., T == to_arrow(to_tiledb(T)))\n9: \n10: Most primitive types are simple -- e.g., uint8. Of particular challenge\n11: are datetime/timestamps as TileDB has no distinction between a \"datetime\" and\n12: a \"timedelta\". The best Arrow match is TimestampType, as long as that\n13: TimestampType instance does NOT have a timezone set.\n14: \n15: Because of our round-trip requirement, all other Arrow temporal types\n16: are unsupported (even though they are just int64 under the covers).\n17: \n18: We auto-promote Arrow's string and binary to large_string and large_binary,\n19: respectively, as this is what TileDB stores -- a sequence of bytes preceded\n20: by a 64-bit (not 32-bit) length int.\n21: \n22: DataFrame-specific note: currently (as of 2.14), TileDB does not support\n23: Unicode array dimensions. All Arrow string types used in a DataFrame index\n24: columns (i.e., TileDB dimension) are coerced to ASCII. This equirement for\n25: ASCII-only dimensions will be relaxed in a future release. Unicode/UTF-8 is\n26: fully supported in SOMA DataFrame non-indexed columns.\n27: \"\"\"\n28: \n29: from typing import Any, Dict, Union\n30: \n31: import numpy as np\n32: import numpy.typing as npt\n33: import pandas as pd\n34: import pyarrow as pa\n35: import tiledb\n36: \n37: _ARROW_TO_TDB_ATTR: Dict[Any, Union[str, TypeError]] = {\n38:     pa.string(): \"U1\",\n39:     pa.large_string(): \"U1\",\n40:     pa.binary(): \"bytes\",\n41:     pa.large_binary(): \"bytes\",\n42:     pa.timestamp(\"s\"): \"datetime64[s]\",\n43:     pa.timestamp(\"ms\"): \"datetime64[ms]\",\n44:     pa.timestamp(\"us\"): \"datetime64[us]\",\n45:     pa.timestamp(\"ns\"): \"datetime64[ns]\",\n46:     #\n47:     # Unsupported types in TileDB type system\n48:     pa.float16(): TypeError(\"float16 - unsupported type (use float32)\"),\n49:     pa.date32(): TypeError(\"32-bit date - unsupported type (use TimestampType)\"),\n50:     pa.date64(): TypeError(\"64-bit date - unsupported type (use TimestampType)\"),\n51: }\n52: \"\"\"Dict of types unsupported by to_pandas_dtype, which require overrides for\n53: use in TileDB Attributes (aka DataFrame non-indexe columns).\n54: \n55: If the value is an instance of Exception, it will be raised.\n56: \n57: IMPORTANT: ALL non-primitive types supported by TileDB must be in this table.\n58: \"\"\"\n59: \n60: # Same as _ARROW_TO_TDB_ATTR, but used for DataFrame indexed columns, aka TileDB Dimensions.\n61: # Any type system differences from the base-case Attr should be added here.\n62: _ARROW_TO_TDB_DIM: Dict[Any, Union[str, TypeError]] = _ARROW_TO_TDB_ATTR.copy()\n63: \"\"\"Same as _ARROW_TO_TDB_ATTR, but used for DataFrame indexed columns, aka TileDB Dimensions.\n64: Any type system differences from the base-case Attr should be added here.\n65: \"\"\"\n66: _ARROW_TO_TDB_DIM.update(\n67:     {\n68:         pa.string(): \"ascii\",  # TODO: temporary work-around until Dimension UTF8 support is available.\n69:         pa.large_string(): \"ascii\",  # TODO: temporary work-around until Dimension UTF8 support is available.\n70:     }\n71: )\n72: \n73: \n74: def tiledb_type_from_arrow_type(\n75:     t: pa.DataType, is_indexed_column: bool = False\n76: ) -> npt.DTypeLike:\n77:     \"\"\"Given an Arrow type, return the corresponding TileDB type as a NumPy dtype.\n78:     Building block for Arrow-to-TileDB schema translation.\n79: \n80:     TileDB currently has different Unicode handling for dimensions and attributes.\n81:     Set the ``is_dimension`` parameter to True for indexed-column (AKA dimension)\n82:     rules, which currently requires all strings to be ASCII.\n83: \n84:     If type is unsupported, with raise a TypeError exception.\n85: \n86:     Args:\n87:         t:\n88:             Arrow DataType instance, e.g., pyarrow.int8().\n89:         is_indexed_column:\n90:             Use TileDB dimension type conversion rules.\n91: \n92:     Returns:\n93:         The numpy dtype corresponding to the ``t`` parameter.\n94: \n95:     Raises:\n96:         TypeError: if the type is unsupported.\n97:     \"\"\"\n98:     if pa.types.is_dictionary(t):\n99:         t = t.index_type\n100: \n101:     arrow_to_tdb = _ARROW_TO_TDB_DIM if is_indexed_column else _ARROW_TO_TDB_ATTR\n102:     if t in arrow_to_tdb:\n103:         arrow_type = arrow_to_tdb[t]\n104:         if isinstance(arrow_type, Exception):\n105:             raise arrow_type\n106:         if arrow_type in [\"ascii\", \"bytes\"]:\n107:             return arrow_type\n108:         return np.dtype(arrow_type)\n109: \n110:     if not pa.types.is_primitive(t):\n111:         raise TypeError(f\"Type {str(t)} - unsupported type\")\n112:     if pa.types.is_timestamp(t):\n113:         raise TypeError(\"TimeStampType - unsupported type (timezone not supported)\")\n114:     if pa.types.is_time32(t):\n115:         raise TypeError(\"Time64Type - unsupported type (use TimestampType)\")\n116:     if pa.types.is_time64(t):\n117:         raise TypeError(\"Time32Type - unsupported type (use TimestampType)\")\n118:     if pa.types.is_duration(t):\n119:         raise TypeError(\"DurationType - unsupported type (use TimestampType)\")\n120: \n121:     # else lets try the default conversion path\n122:     try:\n123:         # Must force into a dtype to catch places where the Pandas type\n124:         # system has extra information that can't be expressed\n125:         return np.dtype(t.to_pandas_dtype())\n126:     except NotImplementedError as exc:\n127:         raise TypeError(\"Unsupported Arrow type\") from exc\n128: \n129: \n130: def arrow_type_from_tiledb_dtype(\n131:     tiledb_dtype: npt.DTypeLike, bytes_are_ascii: bool = True\n132: ) -> pa.DataType:\n133:     \"\"\"Maps a TileDB dtype (``'bytes'``, ``'ascii'``, or an ``np.dtype``) to an Arrow type.  Note that\n134:     when we read tiledb schema off storage, ``ascii`` and ``bytes`` both have ``dtype`` of `\"S\"`\n135:     which is equal to ``bytes`` -- so, the caller should disambgiuate.\n136:     \"\"\"\n137:     if tiledb_dtype == \"bytes\":\n138:         if bytes_are_ascii:\n139:             return pa.large_string()\n140:         else:\n141:             return pa.large_binary()\n142:     elif tiledb_dtype == \"ascii\" or tiledb_dtype == str:\n143:         return pa.large_string()\n144:     else:\n145:         return pa.from_numpy_dtype(tiledb_dtype)\n146: \n147: \n148: def tiledb_schema_to_arrow(\n149:     tdb_schema: tiledb.ArraySchema, uri: str, ctx: tiledb.ctx.Ctx\n150: ) -> pa.Schema:\n151:     arrow_schema_dict = {}\n152:     dom = tdb_schema.domain\n153:     for i in range(dom.ndim):\n154:         dim = dom.dim(i)\n155:         name = dim.name\n156:         if name == \"\":\n157:             name = \"unnamed\"\n158:         arrow_schema_dict[name] = arrow_type_from_tiledb_dtype(dim.dtype)\n159: \n160:     # If there are any enumerated-type columns, we'll need to open the array once to get\n161:     # some information from there. If not, we'll need to open the array zero times.\n162:     # Open the array only if we'll need it for enum infos.\n163:     A = None\n164:     for i in range(tdb_schema.nattr):\n165:         attr = tdb_schema.attr(i)\n166:         name = attr.name\n167:         if name == \"\":\n168:             name = \"unnamed\"\n169:         if attr.enum_label is not None:  # enumerated\n170:             if A is None:\n171:                 A = tiledb.open(uri, ctx=ctx)\n172:             info = A.enum(name)\n173:             arrow_schema_dict[name] = pa.dictionary(\n174:                 index_type=arrow_type_from_tiledb_dtype(attr.dtype),\n175:                 value_type=arrow_type_from_tiledb_dtype(\n176:                     tiledb.datatypes.DataType.from_tiledb(info.type).np_dtype\n177:                 ),\n178:                 ordered=info.ordered,\n179:             )\n180:         else:  # non-enumerated\n181:             arrow_schema_dict[name] = arrow_type_from_tiledb_dtype(\n182:                 attr.dtype, attr.isascii\n183:             )\n184:     if A is not None:\n185:         A.close()\n186: \n187:     return pa.schema(arrow_schema_dict)\n188: \n189: \n190: def df_to_arrow(df: pd.DataFrame) -> pa.Table:\n191:     \"\"\"\n192:     Categoricals are not yet well supported, so we must flatten.\n193:     Also replace Numpy/Pandas-style nulls with Arrow-style nulls.\n194:     \"\"\"\n195:     null_fields = set()\n196:     # Not for name, col in df.items() since we need df[k] on the left-hand sides\n197:     for k in df:\n198:         if df[k].isnull().any():\n199:             if df[k].isnull().all():\n200:                 # Special case: Pandas dtype is string, but the values are\n201:                 # math.NaN, for which pa.infer_type fails with \"Could not\n202:                 # convert <NA> with type NAType\".\n203:                 #\n204:                 # Note: with\n205:                 #   anndata.obs['new_col'] = pd.Series(data=np.nan, dtype=np.dtype(str))\n206:                 # the dtype comes in to us via `tiledbsoma.io.from_anndata` not\n207:                 # as `pd.StringDtype()` but rather as `object`.\n208:                 if df[k].dtype == pd.StringDtype() or df[k].dtype.name == \"object\":\n209:                     df[k] = pd.Series([None] * df.shape[0], dtype=pd.StringDtype())\n210:                 else:\n211:                     df[k] = pa.nulls(df.shape[0], pa.infer_type(df[k]))\n212:             else:\n213:                 df[k].where(\n214:                     df[k].notnull(),\n215:                     pd.Series(pa.nulls(df[k].isnull().sum(), pa.infer_type(df[k]))),\n216:                     inplace=True,\n217:                 )\n218:             null_fields.add(k)\n219: \n220:     # For categoricals, it's possible to get\n221:     #   TypeError: Object of type bool_ is not JSON serializable\n222:     # deep within library functions. Debugging reveals that this happens when\n223:     # the df[key].values.ordered is of type np.bool_ rather than Python bool.\n224:     # So, we cast and reconstruct.\n225:     for key in df:\n226:         column = df[key]\n227:         if isinstance(column.dtype, pd.CategoricalDtype):\n228:             if hasattr(column.values, \"categories\"):\n229:                 categories = column.values.categories\n230: \n231:             if hasattr(column.values, \"ordered\"):\n232:                 ordered = bool(column.values.ordered)\n233: \n234:             df[key] = pd.Categorical(\n235:                 values=column, categories=categories, ordered=ordered\n236:             )\n237: \n238:     arrow_table = pa.Table.from_pandas(df)\n239:     if null_fields:\n240:         md = arrow_table.schema.metadata\n241:         md.update(dict.fromkeys(null_fields, \"nullable\"))\n242:         arrow_table = arrow_table.replace_schema_metadata(md)\n243: \n244:     return arrow_table\n\n(End of file - total 244 lines)\n</content>",
            "metadata": {
              "preview": "# Copyright (c) 2021-2023 The Chan Zuckerberg Initiative Foundation\n# Copyright (c) 2021-2023 TileDB, Inc.\n#\n# Licensed under the MIT License.\n\n\"\"\"Conversion to/from Arrow and TileDB type systems. Must be capable\nof representing full type semantics, and correctly performing a\nround trip conversion (e.g., T == to_arrow(to_tiledb(T)))\n\nMost primitive types are simple -- e.g., uint8. Of particular challenge\nare datetime/timestamps as TileDB has no distinction between a \"datetime\" and\na \"timedelta\". The best Arrow match is TimestampType, as long as that\nTimestampType instance does NOT have a timezone set.\n\nBecause of our round-trip requirement, all other Arrow temporal types\nare unsupported (even though they are just int64 under the covers).\n\nWe auto-promote Arrow's string and binary to large_string and large_binary,\nrespectively, as this is what TileDB stores -- a sequence of bytes preceded\nby a 64-bit (not 32-bit) length int.",
